## =============================================================================
## JVM options for Elasticsearch (jvm.options) - documented template
## =============================================================================
##
## What this file is:
##   One JVM flag per line, read at startup by the Elasticsearch launcher.
##   Lines starting with '#' are comments; blank lines are ignored. A line may
##   carry an optional Java-version prefix so a flag only applies to matching
##   runtimes: "17:" (exactly 17), "17-18:" (17 through 18), "17-:" (17 and up).
##   Example: 17-:-Xmx2g
##
## Doc references (accessed 2026-09-28):
##   1. JVM settings (heap sizing, dump paths, GC logging, DNS cache, tmpdir):
##      https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/elasticsearch/jvm-settings
##   2. System configuration (memory lock, file descriptors, ...):
##      https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/system-config.html
##
## Notes / judgments:
##   - Upstream's guidance: do NOT edit the root jvm.options shipped with the
##     package; put custom flags in jvm.options.d/*.options instead (files
##     there are read in lexical order and override). This dotfiles copy is a
##     documented TEMPLATE of the canonical filename - copy it into
##     jvm.options.d/ (e.g. as 10-custom.options) on a real install.
##   - Heap: modern Elasticsearch AUTO-SIZES the heap from node roles and
##     total RAM, so -Xms/-Xmx are often absent from the shipped file. If you
##     set them explicitly: Xms MUST equal Xmx, and never exceed 50% of RAM
##     (the OS page cache needs the rest) or ~26-30 GB (compressed OOPs
##     cutoff). The 2g values below are a dev-workstation template - raise to
##     min(50% RAM, 31g) on a real node, and pair with
##     bootstrap.memory_lock: true in elasticsearch.yml.
##   - GC: G1GC is the documented default collector for current releases.
##   - Every non-comment line below is a real JVM flag; a structural check
##     (optional version prefix + dash-prefixed flag) is run at validation.

## Heap size: minimum (-Xms) and maximum (-Xmx). MUST be equal.
## ELI5: the JVM's memory pool for Elasticsearch. Too small = constant
## garbage collection pauses; too big = starves the OS file cache that
## Lucene relies on. 50%-of-RAM rule, see header.
-Xms2g
-Xmx2g

## Garbage collector: G1GC (documented default).
## ELI5: the algorithm that reclaims unused memory. G1 balances pause times
## and throughput for a search engine's allocation pattern.
-XX:+UseG1GC

## Touch every heap page at startup.
## ELI5: forces the OS to actually back the heap with RAM now, instead of
## faulting pages in later during a query (avoids latency spikes).
-XX:+AlwaysPreTouch

## Thread stack size.
## ELI5: memory reserved per thread stack. 1m is the documented default;
## larger wastes native memory when many threads exist.
-Xss1m

## Print full stack traces even for "hot" exceptions.
## ELI5: the JVM normally stops printing stack traces for exceptions thrown
## very often (as an optimization). Disabling that keeps logs debuggable.
-XX:-OmitStackTraceInFastThrow

## Show helpful NullPointerException messages (JDK 14+).
## ELI5: tells you WHICH variable was null instead of just "NullPointerException".
-XX:+ShowCodeDetailsInExceptionMessages

## Temp directory for JNA and other native scratch files.
## ELI5: Elasticsearch extracts native libraries at runtime; point them at a
## private dir instead of the world-writable /tmp. ${ES_TMPDIR} is set by the
## startup scripts.
-Djava.io.tmpdir=${ES_TMPDIR}
-Djna.tmpdir=${ES_TMPDIR}

## Don't let JNA use system-installed native libs.
## ELI5: use only the JNA bundled with Elasticsearch, so a stray system
## libjnidispatch can't change behavior or introduce a vulnerable version.
-Djna.nosys=true

## Headless mode (no GUI available/needed).
## ELI5: Elasticsearch never opens windows; tell AWT not to try.
-Djava.awt.headless=true

## File encoding: always UTF-8.
## ELI5: guarantees text (field names, queries) is read as UTF-8 regardless
## of the OS locale.
-Dfile.encoding=UTF-8

## Locale providers: SPI first, then COMPAT.
## ELI5: which Java locale data to prefer, so date/number formatting is
## consistent across JDK vendors.
-Djava.locale.providers=SPI,COMPAT

## Heap dump on OutOfMemoryError.
## ELI5: if the JVM dies from running out of memory, write a snapshot of the
## heap first so you can diagnose what ate it.
-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError
-XX:HeapDumpPath=/var/log/elasticsearch

## Fatal error log location (hs_err_pid file on JVM crash).
## ELI5: if the JVM itself crashes (not just OOM), write the native crash
## report here. %p = process id.
-XX:ErrorFile=/var/log/elasticsearch/hs_err_pid%p.log

## GC logging: gc*, gc+age and safepoint events to a rotating log file.
## ELI5: a permanent flight recorder of garbage-collection pauses - the first
## thing you read when diagnosing latency spikes. 32 files x 64m rotation.
-Xlog:gc*,gc+age=trace,safepoint:file=/var/log/elasticsearch/gc.log:utctime,pid,tags:filecount=32,filesize=64m

## Netty: don't use Unsafe / key-set optimizations / recycler.
## ELI5: Netty is the networking library. These three flags trade a little
## raw speed for safety and predictable memory use (Unsafe bypasses JVM
## safety checks; the recycler pools buffers in ways that complicate
## leak detection).
-Dio.netty.noUnsafe=true
-Dio.netty.noKeySetOptimization=true
-Dio.netty.recycler.maxCapacityPerThread=0

## Logging framework tweaks.
## ELI5: don't install a shutdown hook that could deadlock on exit
## (log4j.shutdownHookEnabled); disable JMX lookup of loggers
## (log4j2.disable.jmx); block ${...} lookups in log messages, the
## Log4Shell-class vector (log4j2.formatMsgNoLookups).
-Dlog4j.shutdownHookEnabled=false
-Dlog4j2.disable.jmx=true
-Dlog4j2.formatMsgNoLookups=true

## DNS cache TTLs (Elasticsearch-specific properties, seconds).
## ELI5: how long the JVM caches DNS answers. 60s positive / 10s negative
## keeps node discovery responsive when IPs change (cloud, containers).
-Des.networkaddress.cache.ttl=60
-Des.networkaddress.cache.negative.ttl=10

## Version-gated example (syntax demo; applies the flag only on Java 17+).
## ELI5: prefix "17-:" = "only pass this flag to JVM 17 or newer". Useful
## when one options file is shared across hosts with different JDKs.
# 17-:FILL_IN_VERSION_SPECIFIC_FLAG_IF_NEEDED
